<p>Hungary heads into a closely watched general election on April 12, with Prime Minister Viktor Orbán trying to extend his 16-year rule as Peter Magyar’s Tisza Party emerges as the main challenger. SCMP reported polls showing Fidesz trailing among likely voters. The vote has drawn unusual attention across Europe because it could reshape Hungary’s relationship with the European Union and shift the balance of power within the bloc after years of disputes over democratic standards, rule-of-law concerns and Budapest’s independent foreign policy posture. The campaign has also brought deeper arguments over national identity and religion into the open. Politico reported that Orbán has cast himself as a defender of Christian values, while former ally and Methodist pastor Gábor Iványi rejects that message, calling it a political use of Christianity rather than an expression of biblical teaching. Beyond domestic politics, the election matters internationally because analysts cited by SCMP say an Orbán defeat could weaken China’s influence in Europe. NPR and other outlets describe the contest as a referendum not just on Orbán’s leadership but on Hungary’s democratic direction, giving voters a chance to decide whether the country stays on its current path or chooses political change.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/znPRRSGOfF0tqzATHJzinMXm1GzrLYVNXdyC5CZ3Am4XD1rOZI0zWW3BhFb0WrGppXSj5lyladpxCvA-4PrBTsBMPi692vy05Q6IgBOEcEUiAc8damw45cI-eul9FvHaAToT6UTSA2tbHmOAOnHravcSK6mzJtea50vMpfWbwg' alt='Viktor Orbán ahead of Hungary’s closely watched parliamentary election.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Poll gap: SCMP reported recent polls showing Tisza ahead of Fidesz by 19 to 23 points among likely voters ahead of Sunday’s vote.</li><li>Social network: Iványi leads an organization that runs homeless shelters, hospitals and schools for poor people, making his clash with the government significant well beyond church politics.</li><li>European ripple effect: SCMP said many see the result as a referendum on the strength of Europe’s right, not just on Orbán’s position at home.</li></ul><blockquote>Has nothing to do with the Bible, with the essence of the Bible. - Gábor Iványi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Viktor Orbán: Orbán is campaigning as the leader who will protect traditional Christian values as he seeks another term. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/hungary-viktor-orban-christian-nationalism-election/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Gábor Iványi: The Methodist pastor and former Orbán ally rejects the government’s Christian-nationalist message and says it does not reflect the Bible’s core teachings. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/hungary-viktor-orban-christian-nationalism-election/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Democracy analysts: Experts interviewed by NPR say the vote will test whether Hungarian voters still have a viable electoral path to replace a dominant governing system. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/05/nx-s1-5770205/will-hungarys-far-right-leader-viktor-orban-be-voted-out-of-power'>NPR</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/05/nx-s1-5770205/will-hungarys-far-right-leader-viktor-orban-be-voted-out-of-power'>Will Hungary&#x27;s far-right leader Viktor Orban be voted out of power?</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3349109/orban-brink-could-hungarys-election-dent-chinas-influence-europe'>Orban on the brink: could Hungary’s election dent China’s influence in Europe?</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/hungary-viktor-orban-christian-nationalism-election/'>Hungary’s Orbán is selling Christian nationalism. His former pastor isn’t buying it.</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/hungary-prepares-to-vote-as-orbans-future-hangs-in-balance'>Hungary prepares to vote as Orban’s future hangs in balance</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/hungarys-orbn-long-annoyed-european-union-now-hope-131756818'>Hungary&#x27;s Orbán has long annoyed the European Union. Now some hope he faces defeat</a> - abcnews.com</li></ul>